  "content": {
    "id": "p48-q21-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Solve the equation $\\log_5(x)+\\log_x(5)=1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No real solution.\n\nChange to base $5$ and let $t=\\log_5(x)$. The resulting quadratic $t^2-t+1=0$ has discriminant $-3$.",
      "worked": "The original equation requires $x>0$ and $x\\ne1$.\n\nBy change of base, $\\log_x(5)=\\dfrac{1}{\\log_5(x)}$. Let $t=\\log_5(x)$, where $t\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}t+\\frac1t&=1\\\\t^2+1&=t\\\\t^2-t+1&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe discriminant is $(-1)^2-4(1)(1)=-3<0$, so there is no real value of $t$ and hence no real solution for $x$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 62
  },
